{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgPEHtpbaMd484VjnUjNj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I graded F3 **true, not planted** (problem: none) and saved it to `grade.json`.\n\n**Why it isn't planted:** none of the six problems in the key (P1–P6) covers late payment or money tied up in unpaid invoices.\n\n**Why it's still true:** the truth tables back up the finding's main pattern.\n- **Customer habits are real.** Every customer has a built-in payment habit, and customers 6 and 7 are both in the 20-day group (t408). The habit groups (0, 3, 8, 20 and 45 days) show median lateness of 0, 4, 10, 24 and 53 days (t410). That fits the finding's split into \"customers 6 and 7\" and \"a broad background tail\". Customer 36, which the finding calls the clear exception, is in the 45-day group.\n- **The above-PO effect holds even after allowing for each customer's habit.** Within every habit group, invoices billed above the PO price are paid more than 30 days late 62–89% of the time, against 15–79% for correctly priced ones (t411). Their median lateness is 37–87.5 days, against 0–50.\n\n**What I couldn't check:** the $113,095/yr figure is a carrying cost at the analyst's assumed 8% cost of capital. The truth tables have no dollar figure for late payment, so I couldn't confirm the size.\n\nThe above-PO invoices may be linked to P4, the steel surcharges. I didn't test that, and the finding is about late payment rather than lost margin, so it doesn't change the grade."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":660,"cache_read_input_tokens":30935,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":660},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id"